course: dictionary
lesson: 8352
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. Rosemary is one of them. It can be a small bush that cooks use as a herb, or it can be a girl's name. Look at the words around it to find out which.
explain: «Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finch beaks on the Galapagos for about 40 years.» Two people are named, Peter and Rosemary, and the capital R shows a name. Finch beaks are not measured as plants, so here Rosemary is a name.
explain: Try the swap check. Put the plant meaning into the sentence: «Peter and a small bush Grant measured finch beaks» makes no sense, so this Rosemary must be a name. When the meaning you try fits the whole sentence, you have found the right one.
fact: Rosemary is a noun with two meanings: a small evergreen bush with strong, fresh-smelling leaves, and a girl's name.
fact: As a herb, rosemary flavours roast lamb, potatoes and bread.
fact: Rosemary is easy to grow in gardens, and bees love it.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, people wear rosemary on Anzac Day to remember soldiers who died.
fact: A name starts with a capital letter, as in «Rosemary Grant».
exercise: «Peter and Rosemary Grant watched the finches' beaks change within just a few years after a drought.» What is Rosemary in this sentence? | *A girl's name | A herb for cooking | A small evergreen bush | A kind of finch
exercise: Mum put a sprig of rosemary in the roast lamb. What does rosemary mean here? | *A herb with strong, fresh-smelling leaves | A type of finch | A girl's name | A soldier who died
exercise: Look at «Rosemary» in «Peter and Rosemary Grant». What does the capital R tell you? | It shows the word is a question | It shows the word is very loud | It shows the word is spelled wrongly | *It shows the word is a name, not a plant
test: «The girl sitting next to me is called Rosemary.» What does Rosemary mean here? | *A girl's name | A small bush with needle-like leaves | A finch from the Galapagos | A roast dinner
test: In Australia, people wear rosemary on Anzac Day to remember soldiers. What does rosemary mean here? | A type of soldier | *A plant with fresh-smelling leaves | A girl's name | A bird
exam: «Peter and Rosemary Grant measured finch beaks for about 40 years.» Which meaning fits, and how do you know? | A herb, because people cook with it | *A girl's name, because two people are named and Rosemary has a capital letter | A bush, because it grows in gardens | A finch, because beaks are measured
